Until now the code that I've built was merely a morphological representation of how the grain growth occurs upon solidification. The values of the parameters used in the code were not referencing any true or physical value with respect to any material since we did not even settle on the material to simulate first. All values were acting as purely CA algorithmic stabilizers and hence didn't have any physical significance. This includes the material property parameters as well as the simulation parameters of dx,dt etc.
This part will be used to actually adjust those values to the ones that actually represent a real material and have physical significance. For this, we need to decide upon the simulation parameters as the source variable are defined in terms of cell size and hence changing the grid size will disturb the whole physics. Thus, we need to shift from such cell based parameterisation to a physical value based system where changing the simulation's resolution scale won't disturb the actual physics everytime. This will be beneficial for optimisation of the resulting CA solver eventually.
Later on, we will introduce true material values from references of research papers where the values are not heuristic anymore but experimentally obtained. Examples of such variables are alpha, cooling coeff, soruce strength, boundary conditions, etc.
